confirmed.evidence:
dag.rs:2343-2352 documents an invariant the code does not implement:
    /// path (Phase 2.8 / M-08): `sys:validators` only changes when a slash
    /// executes, and `invalidate_validators_cache()` is called from `add_vertex`
    /// right after a block is persisted — the only moment the active set can
    /// change in normal operation.
There is no `invalidate_validators_cache()` call in `add_vertex`, and none anywhere in `sync/`. The single production call is dag.rs:1279, inside the `if let Ok(block_json) = serde_json::to_string(&new_block)` branch of the LOCAL build:
    self.invalidate_validators_cache();
    println!("📦 Created Block #{} (Hash: {:.8})", ...);
A node that is catching up (PI's role in this incident) builds no blocks, so it never reaches line 1279 and its cached validator set is frozen at whatever it read first.

ordering.rs:900-902 states the required property explicitly — "every node computes the same leader for the same round GIVEN THE SAME VALIDATOR SET" — and the cache is precisely what breaks the antecedent.

consensus/consensus/src/dag.rs:1105-1110:
    // RE-AUDIT HIGH: slash evidence is gathered by the PROPOSER from its
    // local view and carried by the block; every node verifies it
    // independently before applying (executor::apply_slash_evidence).
    let slash_evidence = executor.collect_slash_evidence();
Independent verification makes the evidence SAFE; it does not make it IDENTICAL. sync/src/lib.rs:1068-1070 asserts the stronger property that does not hold:
    // RE-AUDIT HIGH: the block's own slash evidence, verified by
    // the executor — identical on every node.
It is identical only for a block that is IMPORTED. For a block that is BUILT — which every validator does for every height — it is `collect_slash_evidence()`, i.e. per-node storage:
    core/executor/src/lib.rs:2178  for (key, val) in self.db.scan_prefix("sys:equiv_seen:") {
    core/executor/src/lib.rs:2195  for (_key, val) in self.db.scan_prefix("sys:downtime_attestation:") {
Both prefixes are populated exclusively by gossip receipt (dag.rs:1677, dag.rs:1795, dag.rs:2038).

lib.rs:2178-2190:
```
for (key, val) in self.db.scan_prefix("sys:equiv_seen:") {
    if out.len() >= 5 { break; }
    ...
    out.push(serde_json::json!({
        "kind": "equivocation", "offender": off, "round": round, ...
    }).to_string());
}
```
Two separate nondeterminisms in five lines: (a) the SET returned by `scan_prefix` is this node's local gossip state, and (b) `if out.len() >= 5 { break; }` truncates in RocksDB lexicographic order over `sys:equiv_seen:{offender}:{round}` where `round` is NOT zero-padded (`:100` sorts before `:99`), and the equivocation loop consumes the 5-item budget BEFORE the downtime loop at lib.rs:2195 — so how many downtime items survive is a function of the local equivocation-row count too.

refuted.divergence_sequence:
T0: The local commit loop reaches dag.rs:1113, executes height H under BLOCK_EXECUTION_LOCK, and writes `sys:last_executed_height = H` (core/executor/src/lib.rs:1977). It has NOT yet reached `save_block_json` at dag.rs:1222, so `block_{H}` does not exist in storage.

T1: The ChainSync task is inside process_blocks with the peer's block for H — which is a DIFFERENT block, for either of the two reasons above. `execute_block_parallel_at` returns `AlreadyExecuted { last_executed: H }` (core/executor/src/lib.rs:1672).

T2: sync/src/lib.rs:1089 reads `block_{H}` to decide fork-vs-race. It is still absent, so `stored == None` and control falls to the catch-all at line 1106:
    _ => {
        last_processed = last_processed.max(block.header.height);
        continue;
    }
The peer's block is neither persisted nor rejected; the batch keeps going as if this height agreed.

T3: The local loop finishes and persists ITS block at H via save_block_json (dag.rs:1222), atomically setting latest_height=H and latest_block_hash=hash(B_local).

T4: ChainSync moves to the peer's block H+1. sync/src/lib.rs:1032-1044 resolves prev_hash by reading `block_{last_processed}` = `block_{H}` from storage — which now returns B_local, not B_peer. validate_block (sync/src/lib.rs:269-274) fails:
    return Err(format!("Parent hash mismatch at {}: exp {}, got {}", expected_height, prev_hash, block.header.prev_hash));
and `break`s out of the batch.

T5: This repeats every sync tick, forever. handle_sync_request (sync/src/lib.rs:1203) serves strictly `(req.from_height + 1)..=end_height`, so the peer NEVER offers a block at or below the local tip. The reorg/rollback branch at sync/src/lib.rs:951-1018 is therefore unreachable through this transport, and the node has no path back to the majority chain. That is exactly the observed PI signature: divergence latched at 5077, hard stop at 6063, 23,935 identical Parent-hash-mismatch rejections.

Which block wins on disk at H is decided purely by which of the two paths grabbed BLOCK_EXECUTION_LOCK first on that machine — an ordering that is different on every node, so a single content divergence is amplified into a permanent per-node chain split rather than being detected at H.

refuted.evidence:
sync/src/lib.rs:1080-1087 states the assumption that this incident disproves:
    // (a) BENIGN RACE — the local commit loop executed this height
    //     moments ago and has not persisted its block yet, so the
    //     hash-equal dedup above could not see it. Deterministic
    //     execution means the peer's block equals ours.
"Deterministic execution means the peer's block equals ours" is the exact claim broken by the two findings above, and this branch is the only place the mismatch could still have been caught before the chain split. The fork detector at line 1097 is gated on `stored` being present:
    Some(h) if h != block.header.hash => { ... break; }
so precisely in the window where the local block is executed-but-unpersisted — the window this branch exists to describe — a genuine fork takes the benign path. `sys:last_executed_height` is also never reset by `rollback_to_height` (sync/src/lib.rs:514-549 deletes block records and rewrites latest_height/latest_block_hash only), so even if the reorg branch were reachable, every re-imported fork block would come back as AlreadyExecuted with `stored == None` and land in this same catch-all.

core/executor/src/lib.rs:2295-2300:
```
if reason == "downtime" {
    let prefix = format!("sys:downtime_attestation:{}:{}:", offender, round);
    for (k, _) in self.db.scan_prefix(&prefix) {
        let _ = self.logged_delete(&k);
    }
}
```
`logged_delete` (executor:1084) unconditionally calls `log_block_write(key, None)`, so each scanned key becomes an entry in the BTreeMap folded at executor:1949-1957. The key set comes from `self.db.scan_prefix`, whose contents are written only by DagConsensus::handle_remote_attestation (dag.rs:2035-2054) on gossip arrival — i.e. local history, not block data.

